Thanks for responding.  Actually, upon pulling the motherboard there
was an obvious fault on the back.  The old ribbon cable had managed to
get tucked up behind it a little bit, and when it burned it put burned
gunk all over the back of the connector (the pin tips that show
through the motherboard); among that was some strands of copper that
remained there when the cable was removed.

I cleaned the motherboard off with 91% alcohol and swabs (and a
toothbrush on the connector), built another new cable with good 3-M
connectors (and armored it in that corrugated plastic tubing they use
in cars; no more abrasion), and it works.  At this point I've tested
with all peripherals reinstalled, and NT (which the system came with)
booted up and running screensaver for several hours (not a hard test
but all I had time for).  The only notable fault is that some of the
pixels on the OCP tend to flicker, and the faint eau de burnt plastic
that I was unable to completely eliminate (should have used febreze!).

Next step is to install VMS and run some real tests.  I'm also going
to spend the bucks to add more memory (its at 128, I want at least
256MB).  Did some surfing today and the best price I've found for sets
actually sold for the AS600 is $159/128MB or $304/256MB (these are the
sets of 8 SIMMs).  Hopefully more generic solutions are available at
better prices.

Rich



