Just picked up a used (as-is) AS600 that was supposed to be
operational.  I pulled the cover just to be sure everything was tight
and in place.  The OCP did not light up on powerup (should it on this
model?)  About 20-30 seconds after powerup, about the time the first
screen messages appeared (showing no errors), it started putting out
an impressive quantity of smoke.  Powered down immediately.

Upon checking, the ribbon cable that runs from the OCP to the
motherboard OCP connector (J22) had its #1 and 2 conductors burn the
insulation, and the motherboard connector was quite toasted.  There is
some surface discoloration of the surrounding area, but no sign of the
motherboard itself toasting (at least on the visible side).  The OCP
end of the cable (and the OCP itself) is not damaged for the first
14-15 inches, and its possible the cable was damaged, causing a short,
but there is no way to tell now.  There is no indication that the
cable was damaged or shorted anywhere on its length prior to burning.

Until I put together a new cable, I'm just guessing, but hopefully
someone out there with a service manual or prior experience can
provide some insight; I'm hoping I haven't got a nice new boat anchor.
 Is this a known failure on these models?  Is there anything other
than an external cable short (which can no longer be verified) that
could cause a failure of this type?

Of course... this was going to be a system left running 24 hours a
day.  Even if a new cable fixes it it'll be awfully hard trusting it
again.

Rich Jordan

BTW, while getting the burned cable unstuck, the red-black wire pair
got disconnected from the motherboard (I had planned on marking it
before doing that); its not keyed.  Anyone with an AS600 care to let
me know the proper polarity (red or black wire up)?


