news:3D30F9C9.67551D49@pcde.inka.de...
> Peter da Silva wrote:
> >
> > Is that sentence syntactically sound? I didn't think IA64 and affordable
> > were allowed in the same sentence without a negation operator.
>
> SPARC's aren't cheap either, but low end Sun's sell for less
> than just the OpenVMS operating system license for a workstation.

Well, there's 'expensive and then there's 'inexpensive' and then there's
'commodity-level pricing'...

You can indeed purchase a SPARC workstation for as little as about $1K
(that's definitely 'inexpensive', almost verging upon 'commodity-level
pricing'), whereas the best price you can get an Itanic2 processor at (if
you're willing to buy 1000 of them) seems to be over $1.3K ('expensive',
especially given that it's the runt of the litter:  if you want the fast
version, it's about $4.2K per unit in thousand-unit batches).  The VMS
license you mentioned (ISTR something like $1200) unfortunately also
qualifies as 'expensive', since even though it's far less than VMS used to
cost it must compete with systems like Linux, *BSD, and Solaris that can be
obtained free of charge.

Sun has in the past moved its higher-end SPARCs down into low-cost
workstations/server (seems to add an 'i' suffix - e.g., USIIIi is about to
arrive to replace USIIi in that space), and presumably can still at least
break even on the processors themselves.  Since IIRC a USIII die is well
under half the size of (and consumes less than half as much power as) an
Itanic die and doesn't have billions of dollars in already-sunk costs it
needs to try to start repaying, it's hardly realistic to expect Itanic to be
able to compete with it on purely a cost basis.

Hammer's pricing will be strictly commodity-level (though high-end
commodity-level initially).  It will debut in the Madison (not McKinley)
process generation and thus be only about 1/4 McKinley's die size, and
should be well under 1/2 McKinley's power consumption.

