Ulli Woelfel wrote:
> 
> Hi!
> 
> I have recently got a cheap Alphaserver 4000 5/400 with a BA356 Storage
> Array which is connected to an qlogic ISP1020 SCSI-Controller. The
> problem is that, although I have 6 RZ29B-VW Disks for it, it only runs
> with 4 of them (the other two seem broken) and I can only access those
> in the first an third slot of the BA356 under Linux. When I try to write
> to one of the others, the system gets blocked. However, when exchanging
> the third with either the second or fourth, I can access that disk. So
> it seems there is a Problem with the SCSI-Termination, but where? How
> can I set it up correctly?
> 
> Thanks in advance for your answers!
> 
> Ulli Woelfel
> <woelfel@informatik.mu-luebeck.de>

You need to check the BA356 bus configuration.

With the BA356 unplugged from the wall (AC, mains, etc.) and the
computer, remove the two fan housings from the rear of the BA356.
Behind slot 6 there is a 96 (IIRC) pin socket that contains a
plug-in circuit board.

There are two types of plug-ins:

    1) a "turn-around" jumper

and

    2) an active SCSI terminator.

The SCSI backplane in the BA356 connects slots 0, 2, 4, and 6
together as a bus. It also connects slots 1, 3, and 5 as a second
bus.

If the socket on the backside (under the fan) contains a "turn-around"
jumper then all seven slots are physically connected as one parallel
SCSI bus.

If the terminator module is in the socket then the BA356 has 2 SCSI
busses: slots 0, 2, 4, 6,  and slots 1, 3, 5. The terminator
terminates BOTH busses independently.

I had you take off both fans above because there is also a
socket behind slot 1. This socket is a storage socket for the unused
module that plugs into the socket behind slot 6. In other words,
if you find a jumper module behind slot 6 you should have a
terminator module behind slot 1. Likewise, if you have a terminator
in the socket behind slot 6 you should have a jumper module behind
slot 1.

The socket behind slot one is not connected to anything on the
backplane. It is just a storage area for the unused module.

Which SCSI personality module are you using with the BA356?

HTH,

