Ulli Woelfel wrote:

> Hi!
> 
> I have recently got a cheap Alphaserver 4000 5/400 with a BA356 Storage
> Array which is connected to an qlogic ISP1020 SCSI-Controller. The
> problem is that, although I have 6 RZ29B-VW Disks for it, it only runs
> with 4 of them (the other two seem broken) and I can only access those
> in the first an third slot of the BA356 under Linux. When I try to write
> to one of the others, the system gets blocked. However, when exchanging
> the third with either the second or fourth, I can access that disk. So
> it seems there is a Problem with the SCSI-Termination, but where? How
> can I set it up correctly?
> 
> Thanks in advance for your answers!
> 
> Ulli Woelfel
> <woelfel@informatik.mu-luebeck.de>

The BA356 can be configured in two ways: single-bus and dual-bus.

Inside the BA356, there are actually two busses.  One bus visits all the 
even numbered slots (the slot nearest the cable entrance is slot 0, the 
slot nearest the power supply [assuming 1 power supply] is slot 6).  The 
other bus visits the odd numbered slots.  So, there are 4 even numbered 
slots and 3 odd numbered slots.  

If you're in dual-bus mode, you simply run two cables to the cable entrance 
(personality module), and there's a dual-terminator on the backplane of the 
box.

If you're in single bus mode, you run one cable (it matters which hole you 
plug it into), and there's a jumper on the backplane that connects the two 
busses.

The jumper or terminator can be found on the backplane.  You have to remove 
the fans to get at them.  There's two sockets on the backplane behind the 
fans: one for the jumper or terminator and a place to store the other.  Try 
swapping them to see if you can get it into single-bus mode.

